INT. EMPTY COURTROOM - EVENING

Rows of oak benches. The jury box sits dark. A single banker's lamp glows on the bench.

ALISTAIR VAN TONGEREN, 50s, prosecutor, rehearses before the empty jury box, one hand on the rail. ROXANNE KIPCHOGE, 40s, defense counsel, sits in the gallery eating sunflower seeds from a paper cone. She does not interrupt.

ALISTAIR
Ladies and gentlemen of the jury.

He pauses to let them settle. No one is there. He lets them settle anyway.

ALISTAIR (CONT'D)
You will hear that my opponent's client is a good man. A family man. A man who coaches Little League.

Roxanne cracks a seed.

ROXANNE
(to herself)
Twelve years.

ALISTAIR
(not turning)
You're not allowed to heckle the dress rehearsal.

ROXANNE
I'm not heckling. I'm counting.

He turns. Leans against the rail.

ALISTAIR
You're early.

ROXANNE
So are you.

ALISTAIR
I'm an early man.

ROXANNE
You're a nervous man.

Alistair straightens his tie. Looks at the empty seats.

ALISTAIR
Ladies and gentlemen. The evidence will show...

ROXANNE
(over him)
The evidence will show a boy with a wrench and a bad alibi.

ALISTAIR
Roxanne.

ROXANNE
Alistair.

Silence. She drops the cone. Stands.

ROXANNE (CONT'D)
My client didn't do it.

ALISTAIR
Then prove it.

ROXANNE
That's your job to disprove, remember?

ALISTAIR
(under his breath)
God help us both.

He gathers his notes. She walks to the defense table and sits. He sits at the prosecution's. The lamp hums between them.
